#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25dadb3a-0600-58bf-8a51-1c6b0e5a4b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356147} {:answer/id #uuid "57e84b56-0713-5e59-99f6-f53c7b9e107a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356148} {:answer/id #uuid "0a08b21e-404b-5afe-acc1-1ffa6addc3d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356149} {:answer/id #uuid "5a71b241-71f8-53dc-a5f8-f8e1e619f0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356150} {:answer/id #uuid "128ce7f5-6d87-59bb-bc46-e162c40a6ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "128ce7f5-6d87-59bb-bc46-e162c40a6ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356151} :answer-field/id #uuid "9220d2fe-04da-50b9-bba9-6f422d1ff1bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fd89d80-4cad-5b98-abe3-ec15ead33c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-104626" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324d0659-5d73-5aa6-87ca-7874cf9a4d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356154} {:answer/id #uuid "3e737ec6-5dd5-58e6-a23a-6ba3525c79b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356155} {:answer/id #uuid "3d39b97d-3f2c-562e-8c8f-8ff945056df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356156} {:answer/id #uuid "743fa054-dbb6-5a06-9890-b91310ed6079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} {:answer/id #uuid "b074a186-0a99-52b4-be02-91a8c62526de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "743fa054-dbb6-5a06-9890-b91310ed6079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({e}^{2}+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb853897-cc12-5400-8a93-6553a2ba66ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e7200af-dab3-550e-8a78-4c852dc71426" :question/math-academy-id "q-104764" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{(6{x}^{2}-y)}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =6{x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}-{e}^{\\sqrt{t+1}}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-e⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,9-{e}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-e)$ and $(3,9-{e}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,9-{e}^{2})-f(0,e) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {3}^{3}-3\\cdot (9-{e}^{2}))-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}-0\\cdot (-e)) \\\\  & =(54-27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =(27+3{e}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}+9).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb0a12cd-b525-559f-bbd3-844e771f946a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356161} {:answer/id #uuid "159e24eb-e367-55e8-a44f-d43250ae4db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356162} {:answer/id #uuid "9624a752-ba62-5821-8f54-0c70707823af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356163} {:answer/id #uuid "0e537f42-e435-5da6-b479-5eaad3cc78cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356164} {:answer/id #uuid "e6667766-5468-5275-a082-6f670331bb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9624a752-ba62-5821-8f54-0c70707823af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356163} :answer-field/id #uuid "30603ce0-8680-57a7-ba23-e9bde7ec7852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2d1da9e-392d-545b-af49-ed03e60e5fd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124028" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b26360-7ba2-5b96-9f35-f7319670b077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356168} {:answer/id #uuid "0aeb4c1d-6858-5d88-a65d-a8d3a0aabc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356169} {:answer/id #uuid "e9266f2d-96b0-57c2-a23f-7d275d586016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356170} {:answer/id #uuid "be885642-35e9-5776-a190-08c45a9b6f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356171} {:answer/id #uuid "12ae5192-5ddb-5921-b504-2cc063acd0e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be885642-35e9-5776-a190-08c45a9b6f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356171} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e12d8b-38bc-5251-a87b-956f47085f8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8cd02cd8-8b9c-5f00-ab40-0f26ec3c4eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-124032" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "945c16e6-1f3a-5958-a863-2e2ba83174d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131271} {:answer/id #uuid "97d11620-d6cd-5b28-9e01-23ded901eca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131272} {:answer/id #uuid "a86afb61-c67f-58c2-8925-4a7b5b6858e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (2 + \\ln^{2} 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131273} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fa2978-34ae-51b9-bed1-bbbd1ede702e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (2 + \\ln 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131274} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b82307-c40b-5424-b3b5-82f1aa638b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (1 + \\ln^{2} 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86afb61-c67f-58c2-8925-4a7b5b6858e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (2 + \\ln^{2} 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131273} :answer-field/id #uuid "58f459a0-44f1-529a-b15c-99a345858ae0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73c892c9-a1e1-5820-84c2-50c996c14b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-39349" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$ for $x\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{1}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+{ln}^{2}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
